  "ground_truth": {
    "root_cause": "A one-off backfill job ('recompute_ltv', ~120M row updates on the orders table) generated write volume far above normal, so the read replicas fell behind - replication lag climbed from under 1s to over 300s. When lag crossed the 300s safety cutoff, analytics-api's fail-safe stopped serving from replicas and failed reads by design; routing that read load onto the primary then raised the primary's latency. The trigger is the un-throttled backfill. Recovery required throttling it so replicas could catch up.",
    "evidence_path": [
      "data-platform INFO at 13:00: backfill 'recompute_ltv' started (~120M row updates on orders)",
      "orders-db-replica replication lag climbs from <1s past 45s, then 210s, then 340s",
      "analytics-api serves stale data, then hits its 300s lag cutoff and fails reads by design",
      "orders-db-primary read load +300% as replicas leave rotation and its latency rises",
      "recovery after the backfill is throttled to 10% rate"
    ],
    "fix": "Throttle the backfill (done). Run large backfills in rate-limited batches with replica-lag backpressure (pause when lag exceeds a threshold), schedule them off-peak, and consider a dedicated replica for batch and analytics load.",
    "should_escalate": false
  }
